\markup {
  \column {
    \pad-around #1
    \wordwrap \abs-fontsize #10 {
      \italic { The Art of Fugue, } BWV 1080, is Johann Sebastian Bach's last major work of counterpoint:
      an exploration, taken about as far as it can go, of the possibilities latent in a single musical
      subject.
      The collection comprises fourteen \italic { Contrapuncti } (fugues in various guises: simple,
      counter‐fugues, double and triple fugues, and mirror fugues — their formal plans are mapped out in
      the \italic { Structure of the Fugues } section) together with four canons, all built on the same
      subject or on transformations of it (catalogued in the \italic { List of Subjects } that follows).
    }
  }
}

\markup {
  \column {
    \pad-around #1
    \wordwrap \abs-fontsize #10 {
      Bach appears to have begun assembling the cycle in the early 1740s, continuing to revise, reorder
      and add to it until close to his death in 1750.
      No autograph title survives for the whole collection: the name \italic { Die Kunst der Fuge } was
      most likely chosen by his son Carl Philipp Emanuel, who oversaw its posthumous publication in 1751.
      A second, largely unsuccessful print followed in 1752; unsold copies and the engraved plates were
      eventually sold off for their scrap value, a fate hard to reconcile with the stature the work
      enjoys today.
    }
  }
}

\markup {
  \column {
    \pad-around #1
    \wordwrap \abs-fontsize #10 {
      The work is written throughout in open score, one staff per voice, without any indication of the
      instrument (or instruments) it was intended for.
      This has fuelled a long‐standing debate as to whether Bach conceived it for a keyboard instrument,
      for an ensemble, or primarily as a work of speculative, “eye” music not necessarily meant for
      performance at all.
      The harpsichordist Gustav Leonhardt made an influential case for the keyboard in his 1952 study
      \italic { “The Art of Fugue: Bach's Last Harpsichord Work — An Argument”, } showing that the entire
      score can be played with two hands, and that the bass voice, which occasionally rises above the
      tenor, was never meant to be doubled at sixteen‐foot pitch — evidence he read as ruling out the
      organ and pointing to the harpsichord.
      Reducing the four voices onto a single grand staff for keyboard, as done in this edition, follows a
      well‐established performing tradition stretching back to the nineteenth century, and remains one of
      the most natural ways of experiencing the piece at the instrument.
    }
  }
}

\markup {
  \column {
    \pad-around #1
    \wordwrap \abs-fontsize #10 {
      The order in which the pieces are usually printed — and in which they appear in this edition — is an
      editorial one, arranged by increasing contrapuntal complexity rather than following the order found
      in Bach's own working manuscript (the so‐called Berlin autograph, D‐B Mus. ms. Bach P 200), which
      contains an earlier and incomplete state of the cycle.
      Grouped this way, the four simple fugues on the subject and its inversion are followed by three
      counter‐fugues (stretto fugues), then by the double and triple fugues, the two mirror fugues — each
      notated once and then again with every voice and interval inverted — and finally the great,
      unfinished quadruple fugue, Contrapunctus XIV.
      The four canons close the collection.
    }
  }
}

\markup {
  \column {
    \pad-around #1
    \wordwrap \abs-fontsize #10 {
      Contrapunctus XIV breaks off after 239 bars, in the middle of its third subject — the famous
      \bold { B–A–C–H } motif, spelling out the composer's own name in German note names.
      Whether Bach's failing health simply prevented him from finishing a quadruple fugue he had already
      planned, or whether the piece was left incomplete for other reasons, remains one of the enduring
      puzzles of Bach scholarship; the notes following the last Contrapunctus in this edition discuss the
      question, and the fugue's remarkable proportions, in more detail.
    }
  }
}

\markup {
  \column {
    \pad-around #1
    \wordwrap \abs-fontsize #10 {
      The fugue's incompleteness has invited many responses.
      Bach's own publishers, in the first edition of 1751, replaced it with the chorale prelude
      \italic { “Vor deinen Thron tret ich hiermit,” } BWV 668 — included elsewhere in this collection —
      a substitution most later editions have not retained.
      Since Gustav Nottebohm identified the fugue's implicit fourth subject in 1880, several musicians
      have gone further and completed the piece outright: Donald Francis Tovey in 1931, the harpsichordist
      Davitt Moroney for his 1989 recording of the complete cycle, and, more recently, Zoltán Göncz, whose
      reconstruction is discussed in more detail below.
      Most recently still, the pianist Daniil Trifonov wrote and recorded his own continuation of the
      fugue for his 2019 album \italic { “Bach: The Art of Life”. }
      This edition instead follows Bach's manuscript exactly, presenting Contrapunctus XIV just as he
      left it, without completion.
    }
  }
}

\markup {
  \column {
    \pad-around #1
    \wordwrap \abs-fontsize #10 {
      Largely neglected for over a century after its publication, \italic { Die Kunst der Fuge } was
      gradually rediscovered by scholars and performers from the mid-nineteenth century onward, and is
      today regarded, alongside \italic { A Musical Offering, } as the summit of Bach's contrapuntal art —
      and one of the most searching investigations of what a single musical idea can become.
    }
  }
}
